# Standard library: geometry
#
# Points, distances and angles in the plane, triangles, and congruence.
#
# One record per item. A record begins at column 0 with its kind and name; a
# proof cites it as def:stdlib/geometry/<name> or thm:stdlib/geometry/<name>,
# and the prefix must match the kind. Format is described in DATABASE.md.
#
# An item's statement is written in the theorem form of SYNTAX.md: let and
# assume lines with labels, then a `then` line.
#
# Every item carries at least one of:
#   metamath    a set.mm label or labels supply it
#   open        it is cited but unproved and unbridged
#
# Other fields: target, first-used, note, and for the one definition that
# introduces a symbol, symbol and defines.


definition point
  open        The pilot's row reads "Let A be a point. (The plane is a set of
              points with distance and angle defined on it.)", which is a gloss
              and not a statement. What it becomes waits on def:stdlib/geometry/angle: a point
              is a complex number if the angle is, and a member of a TarskiG
              base set if the angle is a congruence. ELABORATION.md.
  metamath    A ∈ ℂ
  first-used  isosceles

definition angle
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  then        ∠PQR ∈ ℝ. ∠PQR ≥ 0.
  symbol      ang
  defines     vx vy cc cc0 csn cdif cc cc0 csn cdif
              vy cv vx cv cdiv co clog cfv cim cfv cmpo
  metamath    set.mm writes this function inline in every theorem about it and
              never names it, so the corpus declares the constant
  first-used  isosceles
  note        The angle the corpus writes is unsigned: ∠PQR is the absolute
              value of ang applied to the two differences, which lands in
              0 to π and makes thm:stdlib/geometry/angle-symmetric true. ang itself is
              signed, in −π to π, as set.mm has it. GEOMETRY.md weighs the
              seven candidate geometries and takes this one.

              This is the only definition in the corpus that introduces a
              symbol; every other names a word for something set.mm already
              has. See GOALS.md decision 12.

definition collinear
  open        Incidence is a primitive of the geometry, and the readable layer
              cannot state what it is. In the plane it says (R − P)/(Q − P) is
              real, and subtraction takes numbers while P, Q and R are points:
              the sorts that stop |CA| reading as a product stop this too. The
              notation carries a target, so a claim of collinearity elaborates;
              what has no readable statement is the equivalence.
  first-used  isosceles
  note        Named by the merge because def:stdlib/geometry/triangle needs a word for what a
              triangle's vertices are not.

definition triangle
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  then        P, Q, R form a triangle ↔ P ≠ Q and Q ≠ R and P ≠ R and
              not (P, Q, R are collinear)
  open        set.mm has no single notion. The three inequalities are stated
              here and the rest is def:stdlib/geometry/collinear, which is open.
  first-used  isosceles
  note        That the three are points is the three let lines, not a clause of
              the statement; the pilot's row wrote it as prose and the sorts
              carry it.

definition congruent
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let P′ be a point                                                   (H4)
  let Q′ be a point                                                   (H5)
  let R′ be a point                                                   (H6)
  then        triangle PQR ≅ triangle P′Q′R′ ↔ |PQ| = |P′Q′|, |QR| = |Q′R′|,
              |RP| = |R′P′|, ∠PQR = ∠P′Q′R′, ∠QRP = ∠Q′R′P′, and
              ∠RPQ = ∠R′P′Q′
  open        six equations; no set.mm item
  first-used  isosceles

theorem distance-symmetric
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  then        |PQ| = |QP|
  metamath    abssub
  target      abssub
  first-used  isosceles

theorem angle-symmetric
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  then        ∠PQR = ∠RQP
  metamath    gangsym3, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/geometry.mm.
              The angle it writes is unsigned, which is what makes this true:
              ang itself is signed, and the two directions differ in sign. Off
              the branch cut that is arginv; on the cut nothing is negated and
              lognegb gives both directions as π. So the proof is in two cases
              and the absolute values agree either way.
  target      gangsym3
  first-used  isosceles

theorem triangle-swap
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H4)
  then        P, R, Q form a triangle
  metamath    gtriswap, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/geometry.mm:
              set.mm states nothing about a triangle, having no notion of one
  target      gtriswap
  first-used  isosceles

theorem triangle-rotate
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H4)
  then        Q, R, P form a triangle
  metamath    gtrirotate, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/geometry.mm.
              A swap inverts the quotient that says the three are collinear; a
              rotation takes it to 1 − (R − Q)/(P − Q), so this one rests on an
              identity of the field rather than on one fact about reciprocals
  target      gtrirotate
  first-used  isosceles
  note        These two replace a thm:triangle-permute whose conclusion, "any
              ordering of P, Q, R forms a triangle", was not a formula. A swap
              and a rotation generate all six orderings, and they are the two
              the isosceles proof cites: one for A, C, B and one for B, C, A.
              Stating one theorem per ordering was the alternative and is five
              items for two uses.

theorem side-angle-side
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let P′ be a point                                                   (H4)
  let Q′ be a point                                                   (H5)
  let R′ be a point                                                   (H6)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H7)
  assume P′, Q′, R′ form a triangle                                   (H8)
  assume |PQ| = |P′Q′|                                                (H9)
  assume ∠PQR = ∠P′Q′R′                                               (H10)
  assume |QR| = |Q′R′|                                                (H11)
  then        triangle PQR ≅ triangle P′Q′R′
  metamath    gsas, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/geometry.mm. An
              axiom in Euclid and in Hilbert, because a synthetic geometry has
              no coordinates to compute with and congruence has to be
              stipulated; over ℂ it is a theorem. The proof is the law of
              cosines at each of the three vertices, six applications in all,
              which are three cyclic rotations of glawcos in each triangle.
              The third side comes first, since equal sides and equal cosines
              make equal squares and a length is not negative; with all three
              sides equal the other two angles follow the other way round.
  target      gsas
  first-used  isosceles
  note        The isosceles proof instantiates A, B, C, A′, B′, C′, which are not
              this statement's variable names. One of the two must change; the
              merge changed neither. See DATABASE.md.
